---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<Base
  title="Spec GTM: AI-Assisted Design Handoff | Cooper Tillotson"
  description="A spec go-to-market strategy for an AI-assisted design-to-engineering handoff feature in Figma. Problem framing, positioning, launch plan, and success metrics."
>
  <section class="case-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Back to portfolio</a>
      <span class="case-tag">Spec Project</span>
      <h1>AI-Assisted Design Handoff for Figma</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">
        A spec go-to-market strategy exploring how Figma could use AI to close
        the design-to-engineering gap, turning design files into shippable specs
        automatically.
      </p>
      <nav class="case-nav">
        <a href="#problem">Problem</a>
        <a href="#segment">Personas</a>
        <a href="#positioning">Positioning</a>
        <a href="#competitive">Landscape</a>
        <a href="#launch">Launch</a>
        <a href="#metrics">Metrics</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="problem">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">01</span>
      <h2>Problem & Opportunity</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>The gap</h3>
          <p>
            Design and engineering hand off work manually today. Figma files get
            exported, redlined, and re-explained in Slack/Jira, losing fidelity
            and context every time. Design intent (spacing, states, interaction
            logic) doesn't survive the handoff, so engineers rebuild from
            guesswork and design QA becomes a bottleneck late in the sprint.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Why now</h3>
          <p>
            Enterprise teams have scaled design systems (component libraries,
            tokens) but the handoff layer hasn't kept pace. It's still
            screenshots and comments. AI can now read a design file's structure
            and generate accurate, context-aware specs (props, states, responsive
            behavior) automatically, closing that gap for the first time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>The opportunity</h3>
          <p>
            An AI-assisted handoff feature turns Figma from "where design lives"
            into "where design ships from," extending Figma's footprint into the
            engineering workflow. That's defensible market expansion (more seats,
            deeper lock-in), not just a nice-to-have feature.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="segment" class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">02</span>
      <h2>Target Segment & Buyer Personas</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Primary segment</h3>
          <p>
            Mid-market and enterprise product teams with 50+ engineers and a
            dedicated design function. These teams already use Figma at scale,
            have invested in design systems (tokens, component libraries), and
            feel the handoff pain most acutely because volume amplifies every
            miscommunication. They're the teams most likely to pay for Dev Mode
            seats today and expand into AI-assisted tooling tomorrow.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Buyer personas</h3>
          <div class="persona-grid">
            <div class="persona-card">
              <div class="persona-icon">D</div>
              <h4>Design Lead / Head of Design</h4>
              <p class="persona-cares"><strong>Cares about:</strong> Design fidelity surviving handoff, reducing QA burden late in the sprint, protecting the system they've built.</p>
              <p class="persona-pain">"We spec everything in Figma and engineers still ask us what the hover state is."</p>
            </div>
            <div class="persona-card">
              <div class="persona-icon">E</div>
              <h4>Engineering Manager</h4>
              <p class="persona-cares"><strong>Cares about:</strong> Spec clarity, fewer Slack interruptions, predictable sprint velocity.</p>
              <p class="persona-pain">"My team spends the first day of every ticket reverse-engineering the design."</p>
            </div>
            <div class="persona-card">
              <div class="persona-icon">V</div>
              <h4>VP Product / CPO</h4>
              <p class="persona-cares"><strong>Cares about:</strong> Shipping velocity, fewer handoff-related bugs reaching QA, tooling consolidation.</p>
              <p class="persona-pain">"We're paying for Figma and two handoff tools and still getting inconsistent builds."</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Why not smaller teams?</h3>
          <p>
            Startups and small teams feel handoff friction too, but they solve it
            with proximity. The designer sits next to the engineer. AI-generated
            specs add the most value when teams are distributed, cross-functional,
            and operating at a scale where synchronous communication breaks down.
            Smaller teams become the expansion wedge after enterprise proves the
            model.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="positioning">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">03</span>
      <h2>Positioning & Messaging</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Positioning statement</h3>
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              For product teams that ship at scale, <strong>Figma AI Handoff</strong> turns design
              files into complete, context-aware engineering specs automatically.
              Unlike manual redlining or standalone handoff tools, it reads your
              design system, understands component relationships, and generates
              the props, states, and responsive behavior engineers actually need,
              all without leaving Figma.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Message by persona</h3>
          <div class="message-grid">
            <div class="message-row">
              <span class="message-persona">Design Lead</span>
              <p>"Your design system already defines the spec. Now Figma reads it so you don't have to explain it twice."</p>
            </div>
            <div class="message-row">
              <span class="message-persona">Eng Manager</span>
              <p>"Every ticket arrives with the full spec: props, states, spacing, and interaction logic. No more guesswork."</p>
            </div>
            <div class="message-row">
              <span class="message-persona">VP Product</span>
              <p>"One tool for design and handoff. Faster builds, fewer QA cycles, less tooling spend."</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Category framing</h3>
          <p>
            This isn't "design-to-code" (a category with trust issues and mixed
            results). It's AI-powered spec generation: the step between design
            and code that currently lives in Slack threads and outdated Confluence
            pages. Framing it as spec intelligence, not code generation, keeps
            expectations grounded and positions the feature as augmenting
            engineers rather than replacing their judgment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="competitive" class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">04</span>
      <h2>Competitive Landscape</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Current alternatives</h3>
          <div class="comp-table-wrapper">
            <table class="comp-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Player</th>
                  <th>What they do</th>
                  <th>Gap</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Zeplin</strong></td>
                  <td>Dedicated handoff/inspect tool, syncs from Figma</td>
                  <td>Static specs, no AI, adds a second tool to the workflow</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Anima / Locofy</strong></td>
                  <td>Design-to-code generation from Figma plugins</td>
                  <td>Skips the spec layer entirely, output quality varies, engineers distrust generated code</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Figma Dev Mode</strong></td>
                  <td>Built-in inspect, measurements, code snippets</td>
                  <td>Manual, no contextual understanding (doesn't know about states, responsive rules, or component logic)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Storybook</strong></td>
                  <td>Component documentation and visual testing</td>
                  <td>Downstream tool, doesn't bridge the design-to-spec gap</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Figma's advantage</h3>
          <p>
            Figma owns the design file. Every competitor in this space is
            working from an export or a plugin integration, which means they're
            always one sync behind and missing context. An AI feature built into
            Figma reads the source of truth directly: layer structure, component
            instances, variants, auto-layout rules, and design tokens. No other
            player can match that depth of access, and it gets stronger as teams
            adopt more of Figma's design system tooling.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Defensibility risk</h3>
          <p>
            The main threat isn't another handoff tool. It's IDE-side AI
            (Copilot, Cursor) getting good enough at interpreting screenshots
            that engineers skip the spec entirely. Figma's counter: structured
            data beats pixel interpretation. A Figma-native AI reads the
            component tree, not a flat image, so it can generate specs that
            are accurate at the property level, not just visually close.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="launch">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">05</span>
      <h2>Launch Plan & Channels</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Phased rollout</h3>
          <div class="phase-grid">
            <div class="phase-card">
              <div class="phase-header">
                <span class="phase-number">1</span>
                <span class="phase-label">Private Beta (8 weeks)</span>
              </div>
              <ul>
                <li>20-30 enterprise design teams, hand-selected from top Dev Mode accounts</li>
                <li>Goal: validate spec accuracy, collect edge cases, refine AI output quality</li>
                <li>Feedback loop: weekly office hours + in-product thumbs up/down on generated specs</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="phase-card">
              <div class="phase-header">
                <span class="phase-number">2</span>
                <span class="phase-label">Public Beta (6 weeks)</span>
              </div>
              <ul>
                <li>Open to all Organization and Enterprise plans with Dev Mode enabled</li>
                <li>Goal: stress-test at scale, build social proof, generate launch content</li>
                <li>Capture beta testimonials and before/after workflow comparisons</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="phase-card">
              <div class="phase-header">
                <span class="phase-number">3</span>
                <span class="phase-label">GA Launch</span>
              </div>
              <ul>
                <li>Config keynote or standalone launch event (Figma has the audience for this)</li>
                <li>Bundled into Dev Mode seats, driving seat expansion into engineering teams</li>
                <li>Enterprise sales motion: "design system ROI" narrative for CPO/VP Eng</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Channel strategy</h3>
          <div class="channel-grid">
            <div class="channel-item">
              <strong>Product-led</strong>
              <p>In-app prompts in Dev Mode inspect panel. "See the AI-generated spec for this component" as a contextual entry point.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="channel-item">
              <strong>Community</strong>
              <p>Figma Community templates showcasing AI Handoff. Design system maintainers as advocates (they benefit most).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="channel-item">
              <strong>Content</strong>
              <p>"Handoff is broken" narrative via blog, YouTube (workflow comparisons), and design Twitter/LinkedIn. Lead with the pain, not the feature.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="channel-item">
              <strong>Enterprise sales</strong>
              <p>ROI calculator: time saved per handoff x tickets per sprint x engineering hourly cost. Make the business case self-serve for champions.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="metrics" class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">06</span>
      <h2>Success Metrics</h2>
      <div class="case-content">
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Leading indicators (first 90 days post-GA)</h3>
          <div class="metrics-grid">
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Activation rate</span>
              <span class="metric-target">Target: 40%+</span>
              <p>% of Dev Mode users who generate at least one AI spec in their first week.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Spec volume</span>
              <span class="metric-target">Target: 5+ / project</span>
              <p>AI specs generated per active project per week. Measures habitual use, not just trial.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Spec accuracy</span>
              <span class="metric-target">Floor: 80%+</span>
              <p>In-product thumbs-up rate on generated specs. Below 80% signals an accuracy problem that kills retention.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Handoff cycle time</span>
              <span class="metric-target">Target: -30%</span>
              <p>Time from design-complete to engineering-starts. Measured via Dev Mode activity timestamps.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>Business outcomes (6-12 months)</h3>
          <div class="metrics-grid">
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Dev Mode seat expansion</span>
              <p>Engineering teams adding Dev Mode seats after AI Handoff launches. The core revenue thesis: AI pulls engineers deeper into Figma.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Enterprise conversion rate</span>
              <p>Free/Pro organizations upgrading to Organization or Enterprise plans to access AI features.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Handoff tool displacement</span>
              <p>Accounts that drop Zeplin or similar tools within 6 months of adoption. Validates the consolidation narrative.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-name">Net revenue retention</span>
              <p>NRR impact from AI-driven seat expansion. The number that tells leadership this feature is a growth lever, not a cost center.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-block">
          <h3>What not to measure (and why)</h3>
          <div class="callout callout--warn">
            <p>
              <strong>Lines of code generated.</strong> This feature generates specs, not code.
              Measuring code output would misframe the value proposition and
              invite unfavorable comparisons with Copilot. The metric that
              matters is whether the spec was accurate enough that the engineer
              didn't need to ask a follow-up question. That's the signal
              that handoff actually worked.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Case hero */
  .case-hero {
    padding: 4rem 0 3rem;
    background:
      radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 50% at 50% -20%, var(--color-accent-subtle) 0%, transparent 60%),
      var(--color-bg);
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    transition: color 0.15s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .case-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 0.3rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .case-hero h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .case-subtitle {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .case-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .case-nav a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.85rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 2rem;
    transition: all 0.15s;
  }

  .case-nav a:hover {
    background: var(--color-accent-subtle);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-light);
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* Section alternation */
  .section-alt {
    background: var(--color-bg-alt);
  }

  /* Case content blocks */
  .case-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .case-block h3 {
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .case-block p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  /* Callout */
  .callout {
    background: var(--color-accent-subtle);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .callout p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .callout--warn {
    background: var(--color-tag-bg);
    border-left-color: var(--color-text-light);
  }

  /* Persona cards */
  .persona-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .persona-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .persona-card {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--color-shadow);
  }

  .persona-icon {
    width: 2rem;
    height: 2rem;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--color-accent-light);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .persona-card h4 {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .persona-cares {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: none;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .persona-pain {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: none;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }

  /* Message grid */
  .message-grid {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .message-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.85rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }

  .message-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .message-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.25rem;
    }
  }

  .message-persona {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 8rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .message-row p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Competitive table */
  .comp-table-wrapper {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--color-shadow);
  }

  .comp-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .comp-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
    padding: 0.85rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-tag-bg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .comp-table td {
    padding: 0.85rem 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .comp-table tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  /* Phase cards */
  .phase-grid {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .phase-card {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--color-shadow);
  }

  .phase-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .phase-number {
    width: 1.75rem;
    height: 1.75rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    border-radius: 50%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .phase-label {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .phase-card ul {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .phase-card li {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .phase-card li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.55rem;
    width: 0.35rem;
    height: 0.35rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* Channel grid */
  .channel-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .channel-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .channel-item {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--color-shadow);
  }

  .channel-item strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .channel-item p {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Metrics grid */
  .metrics-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .metrics-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .metric-card {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px var(--color-shadow);
  }

  .metric-name {
    display: block;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .metric-target {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-accent-subtle);
